;; agent$claude (RFC-039): hands a task — usually a bead — to Claude Code and
;; sees it through, a turn at a time, in a session a person can attach to.
;;
;; Its own loop is the whole agent: brainyard/loops/claude.edn chooses the
;; session and writes the brief (predictor claude/plan), drives it with the
;; claude$* commands, and judges each of its turns (predictor claude/progress).
;; No model turn of its own calls a tool, so its tools are the commands alone,
;; and its :config is what registers them.
;;
;; brainyard reaches Claude Code over ACP (`acp-agent :claude-code`); this one
;; drives the interactive CLI in tmux instead, so the session is on the agent
;; page and can be attached — divergence `claude-agent-in-tmux`.
{:description
 "Hands a task to a Claude Code session and sees it through to a report: it picks or starts the session (in a git worktree of its own for code changes), briefs it, waits out each of its turns, nudges it on, and stops when the work is done or a person must decide. Give it the task as you would a colleague — a bead id, what done looks like, a model or claude CLI options if you want them, a session id to continue one. By default it does not publish: its answer ends with what was committed (commits, files) and the exact commands to push the branch, open the pull request and clean up the worktree — yours to run. Only when your task explicitly says to push or open a PR does the Claude Code session do it — add \"and clean up the worktree\" to have the worktree and local branch removed once published; it never merges. It asks no permission to publish. Takes minutes to hours."

 :instruction-resource "brainyard/prompts/agents/claude.md"

 :loop "brainyard/loops/claude.edn"

 :tools ["claude$*"]
 :deny-tools ["agent$*"]

 ;; two judgements a turn, each read by a person in the end
 :tier :standard

 :config {:claude-tools? true
          :tool-authoring? false
          :hook-authoring? false
          :user-tools? false}}
